### **Linear Scan – Case-by-Case Version**

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findPeakElement(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            // Case 1: First element
            if(i == 0) {
                if(n == 1 || nums[i] > nums[i+1]) {
                    return i;  // peak found at first element
                }
            }

            // Case 2: Last element
            else if(i == n-1) {
                if(nums[i] > nums[i-1]) {
                    return i;  // peak found at last element
                }
            }

            // Case 3: Middle elements
            else {
                if(nums[i] > nums[i-1] && nums[i] > nums[i+1]) {
                    return i;  // peak found in the middle
                }
            }
        }

        return -1; // this will never happen, just for completeness
    }
};
```

---

### **Explanation**

1. **First element (`i == 0`)**  
   - Only check **right neighbor**: `nums[0] > nums[1]`.  
   - If array has **one element**, it’s automatically a peak.  

2. **Last element (`i == n-1`)**  
   - Only check **left neighbor**: `nums[n-1] > nums[n-2]`.  

3. **Middle elements (`0 < i < n-1`)**  
   - Must satisfy **both neighbors**: `nums[i] > nums[i-1] && nums[i] > nums[i+1]`.  

